import { pastedLessonDay } from "@/lib/learn/pasted-lesson-day";

const PASTED_CONTENT = "# Day 3 — Navigation and Linking\n\n Yesterday, we learned how to **create routes**.\n\n Today, we're going to learn how users **move between those routes**.\n\n Imagine our dashboard has:\n\n```\n/dashboard\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/settings\n/dashboard/reports\n```\n\n Creating these pages is only half the job.\n\n Users need navigation:\n\n```\nDashboard\nUsers\nSettings\nReports\n```\n\n Next.js provides several tools for this:\n\n - `<Link>`\n- `useRouter`\n- `usePathname`\n- `useSearchParams`\n- `redirect()`\n\n The most important distinction is this:\n\n > **Use `<Link>` for normal navigation. Use programmatic navigation when your code needs to decide where the user goes.**\n\n The official Next.js App Router course uses `<Link>` for client-side navigation and `usePathname()` for active navigation.  Next.js\n\n---\n\n # Section 1 — Navigation with `<Link>`\n\n ## `<Link>`\n\n ### Explanation\n\n The simplest way to let users move between pages is Next.js's `<Link>` component.\n\n Import it from:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n```\n\n Then:\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n  Dashboard\n</Link>\n```\n\n Think of `<Link>` as Next.js's enhanced version of an HTML anchor:\n\n```\n<a href=\"/dashboard\">\n  Dashboard\n</a>\n```\n\n The difference is that `<Link>` is designed for navigation within a Next.js application.\n\n Next.js can perform client-side navigation instead of requiring a traditional full-page refresh, and production builds can prefetch linked routes in appropriate situations.  Next.js\n\n---\n\n ## Example\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nexport default function HomePage() {\n  return (\n    <main>\n      <h1>Welcome</h1>\n\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n        Go to Dashboard\n      </Link>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n When the user clicks the link:\n\n```\nUser\n  ↓\nClick Link\n  ↓\n/dashboard\n  ↓\nDashboard Page\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Building a Navigation Bar\n\n A realistic navigation component could look like:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nexport default function Navigation() {\n  return (\n    <nav>\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard\">Dashboard</Link>\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard/users\">Users</Link>\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard/settings\">Settings</Link>\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n This gives the user three navigation options.\n\n---\n\n ## Dynamic Link Values\n\n You don't always have to hard-code the entire URL.\n\n For example:\n\n```\nconst userId = \"42\";\n\n<Link href={`/dashboard/users/${userId}`}>\n  View User\n</Link>\n```\n\n The resulting URL is:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users/42\n```\n\n This becomes especially useful with dynamic routes.\n\n---\n\n ## Main Section Code Example\n\n Suppose your dashboard has:\n\n```\napp/\n└── dashboard/\n    ├── page.tsx\n    ├── users/\n    │   └── page.tsx\n    └── settings/\n        └── page.tsx\n```\n\n Create:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nexport default function DashboardNav() {\n  return (\n    <nav>\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n        Dashboard\n      </Link>\n\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard/users\">\n        Users\n      </Link>\n\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard/settings\">\n        Settings\n      </Link>\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The relationship is:\n\n```\nDashboard Navigation\n       │\n       ├── /dashboard\n       ├── /dashboard/users\n       └── /dashboard/settings\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Key Takeaways\n\n - `<Link>` comes from `next/link`.\n- Use `<Link>` for normal navigation between application routes.\n- `href` specifies the destination.\n- `<Link>` supports client-side navigation.\n- It is generally preferable to manually using `<a>` for internal Next.js navigation.\n- URLs can be constructed dynamically when necessary.\n\n ## Common Mistakes\n\n ### Mistake 1 — Importing from the wrong package\n\n Incorrect:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"react\";\n```\n\n Correct:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 2 — Using `<a>` for every internal route\n\n This works as normal HTML, but you lose the Next.js navigation behavior provided by `<Link>`.\n\n Prefer:\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n  Dashboard\n</Link>\n```\n\n for internal routes.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 3 — Forgetting `href`\n\n Incorrect:\n\n```\n<Link>Dashboard</Link>\n```\n\n Correct:\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n  Dashboard\n</Link>\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 4 — Using an incorrect URL\n\n If the route is:\n\n```\napp/dashboard/users/page.tsx\n```\n\n the URL is:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n ## Mini Quiz\n\n ### Question 1\n\n Where does `<Link>` come from?\n\n A. `react/link`\\\n B. `next/link`\\\n C. `next/navigation`\\\n D. `next/router`\n\n **Answer: B — `next/link`**\n\n---\n\n ### Question 2\n\n What does this do?\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/settings\">\n  Settings\n</Link>\n```\n\n **Answer:** It creates a link that navigates to `/settings`.\n\n---\n\n ### Question 3\n\n Which is generally preferred for internal App Router navigation?\n\n A. `<Link>`\\\n B. `<script>`\\\n C. `window.location` everywhere\\\n D. `<button>` with no navigation logic\n\n **Answer: A — `<Link>`**\n\n---\n\n # Section 2 — Programmatic Navigation\n\n Sometimes you don't want navigation to happen simply because a user clicked a link.\n\n Sometimes your application needs to make a decision first.\n\n For example:\n\n```\nUser submits login form\n       ↓\nLogin succeeds\n       ↓\nNavigate to dashboard\n```\n\n Or:\n\n```\nUser completes checkout\n       ↓\nOrder created\n       ↓\nNavigate to confirmation\n```\n\n This is called **programmatic navigation**.\n\n---\n\n ## Programmatic Navigation\n\n ### Explanation\n\n Programmatic navigation means:\n\n > Your JavaScript code decides when and where navigation should happen.\n\n For example:\n\n```\nButton\n  ↓\nFunction\n  ↓\nNavigation\n  ↓\nNew URL\n```\n\n This is different from:\n\n```\n<Link>\n  ↓\nUser clicks\n  ↓\nNavigation\n```\n\n You typically use programmatic navigation inside a Client Component when the destination depends on an interaction or event.\n\n---\n\n ## `useRouter`\n\n ### Explanation\n\n `useRouter` is a hook provided by Next.js.\n\n You import it from:\n\n```\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n```\n\n Because `useRouter()` is a React hook used in client-side interactive code, the component needs to be a Client Component:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n```\n\n A simple example:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function LoginButton() {\n  const router = useRouter();\n\n  function handleLogin() {\n    router.push(\"/dashboard\");\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={handleLogin}>\n      Login\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Here's what happens:\n\n```\nUser clicks button\n       ↓\nhandleLogin()\n       ↓\nrouter.push(\"/dashboard\")\n       ↓\nDashboard\n```\n\n The current App Router API uses `next/navigation`; this is different from older Pages Router examples using `next/router`.  Next.js+1\n\n---\n\n ## Useful `useRouter` Methods\n\n ### `router.push()`\n\n Adds a new navigation entry.\n\n```\nrouter.push(\"/dashboard\");\n```\n\n Think:\n\n```\nCurrent page\n     ↓\nNew page\n```\n\n The user can generally use browser Back to return.\n\n---\n\n ### `router.replace()`\n\n Replaces the current history entry.\n\n```\nrouter.replace(\"/dashboard\");\n```\n\n This is useful when you don't want the previous URL to remain as a history step.\n\n A common example is updating search/filter state in the URL.\n\n The official Next.js dashboard tutorial uses `replace()` when updating search parameters so the URL changes without adding a new history entry for every search update.  Next.js\n\n---\n\n ### `router.back()`\n\n Moves backward in browser history:\n\n```\nrouter.back();\n```\n\n This is similar to pressing the browser Back button.\n\n---\n\n ### `router.refresh()`\n\n Requests a refresh of the current route's server-rendered data without necessarily doing a full browser reload.\n\n```\nrouter.refresh();\n```\n\n Use this when you specifically need to refresh the current route's server-rendered content.\n\n---\n\n ## Main Section Code Example\n\n Imagine a dashboard has a logout button.\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function LogoutButton() {\n  const router = useRouter();\n\n  function handleLogout() {\n    // Logout logic would normally happen here.\n\n    router.push(\"/login\");\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={handleLogout}>\n      Log out\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The important idea isn't the logout implementation.\n\n It's this flow:\n\n```\nLogout Button\n      ↓\nLogout Function\n      ↓\nAuthentication Ends\n      ↓\nrouter.push(\"/login\")\n      ↓\nLogin Page\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Key Takeaways\n\n - Programmatic navigation means your code controls navigation.\n- `useRouter` comes from `next/navigation`.\n- `useRouter()` is used in Client Components.\n- `router.push()` navigates to another route.\n- `router.replace()` changes the current history entry.\n- `router.back()` goes backward.\n- `router.refresh()` refreshes the current route's server-rendered data.\n\n ## Common Mistakes\n\n ### Mistake 1 — Forgetting `\"use client\"`\n\n Incorrect:\n\n```\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function Button() {\n  const router = useRouter();\n}\n```\n\n For a component using this interactive hook, make it a Client Component:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 2 — Using `next/router` in an App Router lesson\n\n For the App Router, use:\n\n```\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n```\n\n Do not blindly copy older Pages Router examples using:\n\n```\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/router\";\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 3 — Using `push` when `replace` is more appropriate\n\n If a search box updates the URL on every interaction:\n\n```\n?query=a\n?query=ap\n?query=app\n```\n\n you may not want every intermediate value added to browser history.\n\n `replace()` can be more appropriate.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 4 — Using programmatic navigation for every link\n\n If all you need is:\n\n```\nClick Users → /dashboard/users\n```\n\n you usually don't need a button and `useRouter`.\n\n Just use:\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/dashboard/users\">\n  Users\n</Link>\n```\n\n ## Mini Quiz\n\n ### Question 1\n\n Where does `useRouter` come from in the App Router?\n\n **Answer:**\n\n```\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Question 2\n\n What does this do?\n\n```\nrouter.push(\"/settings\");\n```\n\n **Answer:** It programmatically navigates to `/settings`.\n\n---\n\n ### Question 3\n\n Which method is useful when changing the current URL without wanting to add another history entry?\n\n A. `push()`\\\n B. `replace()`\\\n C. `back()`\\\n D. `refresh()`\n\n **Answer: B — `replace()`**\n\n---\n\n ### Question 4\n\n What should you normally use for a simple navigation link?\n\n **Answer:** `<Link>`.\n\n---\n\n # Section 3 — Reading the Current URL\n\n Navigation isn't only about changing URLs.\n\n Sometimes your application needs to know where the user currently is.\n\n For example:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n A navigation component might want to know:\n\n > \"Is the user currently on the Users page?\"\n\n That's where `usePathname()` comes in.\n\n And if the URL contains:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n you may need `useSearchParams()`.\n\n---\n\n ## `usePathname`\n\n ### Explanation\n\n `usePathname()` lets a Client Component read the current pathname.\n\n Import it:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n```\n\n Then:\n\n```\nconst pathname = usePathname();\n```\n\n If the user is on:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n the pathname is:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n If the user is on:\n\n```\n/dashboard/settings\n```\n\n the pathname is:\n\n```\n/dashboard/settings\n```\n\n Think of it as:\n\n```\nFull URL\n   ↓\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n   ↓\nPathname\n   ↓\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n The official Next.js documentation uses `usePathname()` to determine the current route when implementing active navigation.  Next.js\n\n---\n\n ## Example\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function CurrentPage() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return <p>Current page: {pathname}</p>;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n ## `useSearchParams`\n\n ### Explanation\n\n Search parameters are the values after `?` in a URL.\n\n For example:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin&sort=name\n```\n\n The pathname is:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n The search parameters are:\n\n```\nrole=admin\nsort=name\n```\n\n `useSearchParams()` allows a Client Component to read these values. Next.js documents it as a way to access the current URL's query parameters.  Next.js\n\n Example:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useSearchParams } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function UserFilter() {\n  const searchParams = useSearchParams();\n\n  const role = searchParams.get(\"role\");\n\n  return <p>Role: {role}</p>;\n}\n```\n\n For:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n the result is:\n\n```\nRole: admin\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Search Parameters vs Route Segments\n\n This distinction is extremely important.\n\n Consider:\n\n```\n/products/123?sort=price\n```\n\n There are two different ideas:\n\n```\n/products/123\n      ↑\nRoute\n\n?sort=price\n      ↑\nSearch parameter\n```\n\n Route information describes **which resource/page you're looking at**.\n\n Search parameters often describe **how that page should be filtered, sorted, searched, or displayed**.\n\n For example:\n\n```\n/products/laptops?sort=price&brand=lenovo\n```\n\n Could mean:\n\n```\nRoute:\n /products/laptops\n\nSearch parameters:\n sort=price\n brand=lenovo\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Main Section Code Example\n\n Let's build a simple user filter.\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useSearchParams } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function UserFilter() {\n  const searchParams = useSearchParams();\n\n  const role = searchParams.get(\"role\");\n  const status = searchParams.get(\"status\");\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>Role: {role ?? \"all\"}</p>\n      <p>Status: {status ?? \"all\"}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Given:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin&status=active\n```\n\n the component reads:\n\n```\nrole   → admin\nstatus → active\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Combining `usePathname` and `useSearchParams`\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport {\n  usePathname,\n  useSearchParams,\n} from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function UrlInformation() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n  const searchParams = useSearchParams();\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>Path: {pathname}</p>\n      <p>Query: {searchParams.get(\"query\")}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n For:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?query=alice\n```\n\n you get:\n\n```\nPath:\n /dashboard/users\n\nQuery:\n alice\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Key Takeaways\n\n - `usePathname()` reads the current pathname.\n- `useSearchParams()` reads URL query parameters.\n- Both are App Router hooks from `next/navigation`.\n- These hooks are used in Client Components.\n- Pathname and search parameters are different pieces of the URL.\n- Search parameters are useful for search, filters, sorting, and pagination.\n- URL state can be shareable and bookmarkable.  Next.js\n\n Import it from:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n```\n\n Then:\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n  Dashboard\n</Link>\n```\n\n     Next.js can perform client-side navigation instead of requiring a traditional full-page refresh, and production builds can prefetch linked routes in appropriate situations.  Next.js\n\n---\n\n ## Example\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nexport default function HomePage() {\n  return (\n    <main>\n      <h1>Welcome</h1>\n\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n        Go to Dashboard\n      </Link>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n When the user clicks the link:\n\n```\nUser\n  ↓\nClick Link\n  ↓\n/dashboard\n  ↓\nDashboard Page\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Building a Navigation Bar\n\n A realistic navigation component could look like:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nexport default function Navigation() {\n  return (\n    <nav>\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard\">Dashboard</Link>\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard/users\">Users</Link>\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard/settings\">Settings</Link>\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n This gives the user three navigation options.\n\n---\n\n ## Dynamic Link Values\n\n You don't always have to hard-code the entire URL.\n\n For example:\n\n```\nconst userId = \"42\";\n\n<Link href={`/dashboard/users/${userId}`}>\n  View User\n</Link>\n```\n\n The resulting URL is:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users/42\n```\n\n This becomes especially useful with dynamic routes.\n\n---\n\n ## Main Section Code Example\n\n Suppose your dashboard has:\n\n```\napp/\n└── dashboard/\n    ├── page.tsx\n    ├── users/\n    │   └── page.tsx\n    └── settings/\n        └── page.tsx\n```\n\n Create:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nexport default function DashboardNav() {\n  return (\n    <nav>\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n        Dashboard\n      </Link>\n\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard/users\">\n        Users\n      </Link>\n\n      <Link href=\"/dashboard/settings\">\n        Settings\n      </Link>\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The relationship is:\n\n```\nDashboard Navigation\n       │\n       ├── /dashboard\n       ├── /dashboard/users\n       └── /dashboard/settings\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Key Takeaways\n\n - `<Link>` comes from `next/link`.\n- Use `<Link>` for normal navigation between application routes.\n- `href` specifies the destination.\n- `<Link>` supports client-side navigation.\n- It is generally preferable to manually using `<a>` for internal Next.js navigation.\n- URLs can be constructed dynamically when necessary.\n\n ## Common Mistakes\n\n    > Your JavaScript code decides when and where navigation should happen.\n\n  ## Common Mistakes\n\n ### Mistake 1 — Confusing pathname with query parameters\n\n For:\n\n```\n/products?category=laptops\n```\n\n the pathname is:\n\n```\n/products\n```\n\n The query parameter is:\n\n```\ncategory=laptops\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 2 — Forgetting `.get()`\n\n Incorrect:\n\n```\nconst role = searchParams.role;\n```\n\n `useSearchParams()` returns a URL search parameter object with methods such as:\n\n```\nsearchParams.get(\"role\")\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 3 — Forgetting `\"use client\"`\n\n Hooks such as:\n\n```\nusePathname()\nuseSearchParams()\n```\n\n are client-side navigation hooks.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 4 — Assuming a missing parameter returns a value\n\n For:\n\n```\n/users\n```\n\n this:\n\n```\nsearchParams.get(\"role\")\n```\n\n can return:\n\n```\nnull\n```\n\n So you can provide a fallback:\n\n```\nconst role = searchParams.get(\"role\") ?? \"all\";\n```\n\n ## Mini Quiz\n\n ### Question 1\n\n For this URL:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n what does `usePathname()` return?\n\n **Answer:**\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Question 2\n\n What does this return?\n\n```\nsearchParams.get(\"role\")\n```\n\n for:\n\n```\n/users?role=admin\n```\n\n **Answer:** `\"admin\"`\n\n---\n\n ### Question 3\n\n Which part is the search parameter?\n\n```\n/products/laptops?sort=price\n```\n\n A. `/products`\\\n B. `/laptops`\\\n C. `sort=price`\\\n D. `/products/laptops`\n\n **Answer: C — `sort=price`**\n\n---\n\n # Section 4 — Dynamic and Active Navigation\n\n Real dashboard navigation needs to become smarter.\n\n Imagine you have:\n\n```\nDashboard\nUsers\nReports\nSettings\n```\n\n When you're on:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n you want:\n\n```\nUsers\n```\n\n to be highlighted.\n\n You may also need links whose URLs depend on data.\n\n---\n\n ## Dynamic Navigation\n\n ### Explanation\n\n Dynamic navigation means creating navigation destinations based on values.\n\n Imagine your application contains users:\n\n```\nconst users = [\n  { id: \"101\", name: \"Alice\" },\n  { id: \"102\", name: \"Bob\" },\n];\n```\n\n You could generate links:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nconst users = [\n  { id: \"101\", name: \"Alice\" },\n  { id: \"102\", name: \"Bob\" },\n];\n\nexport default function UserList() {\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {users.map((user) => (\n        <li key={user.id}>\n          <Link href={`/dashboard/users/${user.id}`}>\n            {user.name}\n          </Link>\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The resulting links are:\n\n```\nAlice → /dashboard/users/101\nBob   → /dashboard/users/102\n```\n\n This is extremely common in dashboards, ecommerce websites, and admin panels.\n\n---\n\n ## Active Navigation\n\n ### Explanation\n\n Active navigation tells the user:\n\n > \"You are currently here.\"\n\n For example:\n\n```\nDashboard\nUsers       ← active\nReports\nSettings\n```\n\n To do this, we need to know the current pathname.\n\n That's what `usePathname()` gives us.\n\n The official Next.js dashboard tutorial uses exactly this pattern for active sidebar links.  Next.js\n\n---\n\n ## Example\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function NavLinks() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <nav>\n      <Link\n        href=\"/dashboard\"\n        className={\n          pathname === \"/dashboard\"\n            ? \"active\"\n            : \"\"\n        }\n      >\n        Dashboard\n      </Link>\n\n      <Link\n        href=\"/dashboard/users\"\n        className={\n          pathname === \"/dashboard/users\"\n            ? \"active\"\n            : \"\"\n        }\n      >\n        Users\n      </Link>\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The logic is:\n\n```\nCurrent pathname\n       ↓\nCompare with link\n       ↓\nMatch?\n  /     \\\nYes      No\n ↓       ↓\nActive  Normal\n```\n\n---\n\n ## A More Reusable Version\n\n Instead of repeating the logic, define your navigation data:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nconst links = [\n  {\n    name: \"Dashboard\",\n    href: \"/dashboard\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Users\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/users\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Settings\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/settings\",\n  },\n];\n\nexport default function NavLinks() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <nav>\n      {links.map((link) => {\n        const active = pathname === link.href;\n\n        return (\n          <Link\n            key={link.href}\n            href={link.href}\n            className={active ? \"active\" : \"\"}\n          >\n            {link.name}\n          </Link>\n        );\n      })}\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Now adding a navigation item is easy.\n\n Add:\n\n```\n{\n  name: \"Reports\",\n  href: \"/dashboard/reports\",\n}\n```\n\n and the component automatically handles it.\n\n---\n\n ## Nested Active Routes\n\n Sometimes you want:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/users/101\n/dashboard/users/102\n```\n\n to all activate the Users navigation item.\n\n An exact comparison:\n\n```\npathname === \"/dashboard/users\"\n```\n\n would not work for:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users/101\n```\n\n You could instead use:\n\n```\npathname.startsWith(\"/dashboard/users\")\n```\n\n But be careful.\n\n For example:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/users/101\n```\n\n should probably activate Users.\n\n However:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users-settings\n```\n\n also starts with the same characters.\n\n So in larger applications, active-route matching needs deliberate rules rather than blindly using `startsWith()`.\n\n---\n\n ## Main Section Code Example\n\n A realistic dashboard navigation:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nconst links = [\n  {\n    name: \"Dashboard\",\n    href: \"/dashboard\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Users\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/users\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Reports\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/reports\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Settings\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/settings\",\n  },\n];\n\nexport default function Sidebar() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <aside>\n      <nav>\n        {links.map((link) => {\n          const isActive =\n            pathname === link.href;\n\n          return (\n            <Link\n              key={link.href}\n              href={link.href}\n              aria-current={\n                isActive ? \"page\" : undefined\n              }\n            >\n              {link.name}\n            </Link>\n          );\n        })}\n      </nav>\n    </aside>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Notice this:\n\n```\naria-current={isActive ? \"page\" : undefined}\n```\n\n It can communicate the current page to assistive technologies.\n\n That's a small detail, but it is a good habit when building navigation.\n\n---\n\n ## Key Takeaways\n\n - Dynamic navigation creates URLs using data.\n- `map()` is useful for generating navigation from an array.\n- `usePathname()` can determine the current route.\n- Active links can be styled based on the pathname.\n- Exact matching is simple for top-level routes.\n- Nested routes require more careful matching.\n- Navigation should communicate the current page clearly.\n\n ## Common Mistakes\n\n ### Mistake 1 — Forgetting `\"use client\"`\n\n This component uses:\n\n```\nusePathname()\n```\n\n so it needs to be a Client Component.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 2 — Comparing the wrong URL\n\n If the current URL is:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n but your link is:\n\n```\n/users\n```\n\n this comparison won't match.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 3 — Using broad matching carelessly\n\n This:\n\n```\npathname.startsWith(\"/dashboard/user\")\n```\n\n could match routes you didn't intend.\n\n Use route-aware matching rules.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 4 — Forgetting stable keys\n\n When rendering navigation with `.map()`:\n\n```\nlinks.map((link) => (\n  <Link key={link.href}>\n```\n\n use a stable unique key.\n\n---\n\n ## Mini Quiz\n\n ### Question 1\n\n Why do we use `usePathname()` for active navigation?\n\n **Answer:** To find the current pathname and compare it with navigation links.\n\n---\n\n ### Question 2\n\n Which link should be active for:\n\n```\n/dashboard/settings\n```\n\n if the navigation contains:\n\n```\n/dashboard\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/settings\n```\n\n **Answer:** `/dashboard/settings`.\n\n---\n\n ### Question 3\n\n What is the advantage of storing links in an array?\n\n **Answer:** You can generate the navigation dynamically and add/remove links more easily.\n\n---\n\n ### Question 4\n\n Why might this be problematic?\n\n```\npathname.startsWith(\"/dashboard/user\")\n```\n\n **Answer:** It can match routes that merely begin with that string but aren't necessarily the intended route.\n\n---\n\n # Section 5 — Redirects and Dashboard Navigation Project\n\n Navigation sometimes means:\n\n > \"Go somewhere else because a condition requires it.\"\n\n For example:\n\n```\nUser visits protected page\n        ↓\nIs user authenticated?\n     /       \\\n   Yes        No\n   ↓          ↓\nDashboard   Login\n```\n\n That's where redirects become useful.\n\n---\n\n ## Redirects\n\n ### Explanation\n\n A redirect tells Next.js:\n\n > \"The requested destination isn't where this user should stay. Send them somewhere else.\"\n\n Next.js provides a `redirect()` function for redirects in supported server-side contexts such as Server Components, Route Handlers, and Server Actions.\n\n For example:\n\n```\nimport { redirect } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default async function DashboardPage() {\n  const isAuthenticated = false;\n\n  if (!isAuthenticated) {\n    redirect(\"/login\");\n  }\n\n  return <h1>Dashboard</h1>;\n}\n```\n\n The flow is:\n\n```\n/dashboard\n     ↓\nCheck authentication\n     ↓\nAuthenticated?\n   /       \\\n No         Yes\n ↓           ↓\n/login    Dashboard\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Redirect vs Programmatic Navigation\n\n This distinction is important.\n\n ### Use `<Link>` when:\n\n The user should click a normal navigation link.\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/dashboard\">\n  Dashboard\n</Link>\n```\n\n ### Use `router.push()` when:\n\n A Client Component needs to navigate after an interaction.\n\n```\nrouter.push(\"/dashboard\");\n```\n\n ### Use `redirect()` when:\n\n Server-side application logic determines that the user should be sent elsewhere.\n\n```\nredirect(\"/login\");\n```\n\n A simple mental model:\n\n```\nNormal link\n   ↓\n<Link>\n\nClient interaction\n   ↓\nuseRouter()\n\nServer-side condition\n   ↓\nredirect()\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Dynamic Navigation with Search Parameters\n\n Let's combine navigation with search.\n\n Suppose the dashboard contains:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n and users can filter by role.\n\n You might produce:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n Using `<Link>`:\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/dashboard/users?role=admin\">\n  Admin Users\n</Link>\n```\n\n Or dynamically:\n\n```\nconst role = \"admin\";\n\n<Link href={`/dashboard/users?role=${role}`}>\n  Admin Users\n</Link>\n```\n\n The URL now contains state the user can bookmark or share.\n\n Next.js's own dashboard tutorial uses URL search parameters for search and pagination because the URL can represent the current search state.  Next.js\n\n---\n\n # Project: Dashboard Navigation System\n\n Now let's build the project for Day 3.\n\n ## Project Goal\n\n We'll create:\n\n```\n/dashboard\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/reports\n/dashboard/settings\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n Our navigation should:\n\n - Link between dashboard pages\n- Highlight the current page\n- Generate links dynamically\n- Read search parameters\n- Navigate programmatically when necessary\n- Redirect unauthenticated users\n\n---\n\n```\ncategory=laptops\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 2 — Forgetting `.get()`\n\n Incorrect:\n\n```\nconst role = searchParams.role;\n```\n\n `useSearchParams()` returns a URL search parameter object with methods such as:\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 3 — Forgetting `\"use client\"`\n\n Hooks such as:\n\n```\nusePathname()\nuseSearchParams()\n```\n\n are client-side navigation hooks.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 4 — Assuming a missing parameter returns a value\n\n For:\n\n```\n/users\n```\n\n     So you can provide a fallback:\n\n```\nconst role = searchParams.get(\"role\") ?? \"all\";\n```\n\n ## Mini Quiz\n\n ### Question 1\n\n For this URL:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n what does `usePathname()` return?\n\n **Answer:**\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Question 2\n\n```\n/users?role=admin\n```\n\n **Answer:** `\"admin\"`\n\n---\n\n ### Question 3\n\n Which part is the search parameter?\n\n```\n/products/laptops?sort=price\n```\n\n A. `/products`\\\n B. `/laptops`\\\n C. `sort=price`\\\n D. `/products/laptops`\n\n **Answer: C — `sort=price`**\n\n---\n\n # Section 4 — Dynamic and Active Navigation\n\n  Imagine you have:\n\n```\nDashboard\nUsers\nReports\nSettings\n```\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n```\n\n you want:\n\n```\nUsers\n```\n\n to be highlighted.\n\n---\n\n ## Dynamic Navigation\n\n   Imagine your application contains users:\n\n```\nconst users = [\n  { id: \"101\", name: \"Alice\" },\n  { id: \"102\", name: \"Bob\" },\n];\n```\n\n You could generate links:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nconst users = [\n  { id: \"101\", name: \"Alice\" },\n  { id: \"102\", name: \"Bob\" },\n];\n\nexport default function UserList() {\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {users.map((user) => (\n        <li key={user.id}>\n          <Link href={`/dashboard/users/${user.id}`}>\n            {user.name}\n          </Link>\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The resulting links are:\n\n```\nAlice → /dashboard/users/101\nBob   → /dashboard/users/102\n```\n\n This is extremely common in dashboards, ecommerce websites, and admin panels.\n\n---\n\n ## Active Navigation\n\n ### Explanation\n\n Active navigation tells the user:\n\n > \"You are currently here.\"\n\n For example:\n\n```\nDashboard\nUsers       ← active\nReports\nSettings\n```\n\n To do this, we need to know the current pathname.\n\n That's what `usePathname()` gives us.\n\n The official Next.js dashboard tutorial uses exactly this pattern for active sidebar links.  Next.js\n\n---\n\n ## Example\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function NavLinks() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <nav>\n      <Link\n        href=\"/dashboard\"\n        className={\n          pathname === \"/dashboard\"\n            ? \"active\"\n            : \"\"\n        }\n      >\n        Dashboard\n      </Link>\n\n      <Link\n        href=\"/dashboard/users\"\n        className={\n          pathname === \"/dashboard/users\"\n            ? \"active\"\n            : \"\"\n        }\n      >\n        Users\n      </Link>\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The logic is:\n\n```\nCurrent pathname\n       ↓\nCompare with link\n       ↓\nMatch?\n  /     \\\nYes      No\n ↓       ↓\nActive  Normal\n```\n\n---\n\n ## A More Reusable Version\n\n Instead of repeating the logic, define your navigation data:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nconst links = [\n  {\n    name: \"Dashboard\",\n    href: \"/dashboard\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Users\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/users\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Settings\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/settings\",\n  },\n];\n\nexport default function NavLinks() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <nav>\n      {links.map((link) => {\n        const active = pathname === link.href;\n\n        return (\n          <Link\n            key={link.href}\n            href={link.href}\n            className={active ? \"active\" : \"\"}\n          >\n            {link.name}\n          </Link>\n        );\n      })}\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Now adding a navigation item is easy.\n\n Add:\n\n```\n{\n  name: \"Reports\",\n  href: \"/dashboard/reports\",\n}\n```\n\n and the component automatically handles it.\n\n---\n\n ## Nested Active Routes\n\n Sometimes you want:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/users/101\n/dashboard/users/102\n```\n\n to all activate the Users navigation item.\n\n An exact comparison:\n\n```\npathname === \"/dashboard/users\"\n```\n\n would not work for:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users/101\n```\n\n You could instead use:\n\n```\npathname.startsWith(\"/dashboard/users\")\n```\n\n But be careful.\n\n For example:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/users/101\n```\n\n should probably activate Users.\n\n However:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users-settings\n```\n\n also starts with the same characters.\n\n So in larger applications, active-route matching needs deliberate rules rather than blindly using `startsWith()`.\n\n---\n\n ## Main Section Code Example\n\n A realistic dashboard navigation:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nconst links = [\n  {\n    name: \"Dashboard\",\n    href: \"/dashboard\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Users\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/users\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Reports\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/reports\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Settings\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/settings\",\n  },\n];\n\nexport default function Sidebar() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <aside>\n      <nav>\n        {links.map((link) => {\n          const isActive =\n            pathname === link.href;\n\n          return (\n            <Link\n              key={link.href}\n              href={link.href}\n              aria-current={\n                isActive ? \"page\" : undefined\n              }\n            >\n              {link.name}\n            </Link>\n          );\n        })}\n      </nav>\n    </aside>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Notice this:\n\n```\naria-current={isActive ? \"page\" : undefined}\n```\n\n It can communicate the current page to assistive technologies.\n\n That's a small detail, but it is a good habit when building navigation.\n\n---\n\n ## Key Takeaways\n\n - Dynamic navigation creates URLs using data.\n- `map()` is useful for generating navigation from an array.\n- `usePathname()` can determine the current route.\n- Active links can be styled based on the pathname.\n- Exact matching is simple for top-level routes.\n- Nested routes require more careful matching.\n- Navigation should communicate the current page clearly.\n\n ## Common Mistakes\n\n ### Mistake 1 — Forgetting `\"use client\"`\n\n   so it needs to be a Client Component.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 2 — Comparing the wrong URL\n\n ## Project Structure\n\n```\napp/\n├── login/\n│   └── page.tsx\n│\n└── dashboard/\n    ├── page.tsx\n    ├── users/\n    │   └── page.tsx\n    ├── reports/\n    │   └── page.tsx\n    ├── settings/\n    │   └── page.tsx\n    └── _components/\n        ├── Sidebar.tsx\n        └── LogoutButton.tsx\n```\n\n The URL structure is:\n\n```\n/dashboard\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/reports\n/dashboard/settings\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Step 1 — Dashboard Sidebar\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nconst links = [\n  {\n    name: \"Dashboard\",\n    href: \"/dashboard\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Users\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/users\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Reports\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/reports\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Settings\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/settings\",\n  },\n];\n\nexport default function Sidebar() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <aside>\n      <h2>Admin Dashboard</h2>\n\n      <nav>\n        {links.map((link) => {\n          const isActive =\n            pathname === link.href;\n\n          return (\n            <Link\n              key={link.href}\n              href={link.href}\n              aria-current={\n                isActive ? \"page\" : undefined\n              }\n            >\n              {link.name}\n            </Link>\n          );\n        })}\n      </nav>\n    </aside>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n This combines:\n\n```\n<Link>\n   +\nusePathname()\n   +\ndynamic navigation\n   +\nactive navigation\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Step 2 — Dashboard Layout\n\n```\nimport Sidebar from \"./_components/Sidebar\";\n\nexport default function DashboardLayout({\n  children,\n}: {\n  children: React.ReactNode;\n}) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <Sidebar />\n\n      <main>\n        {children}\n      </main>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The hierarchy becomes:\n\n```\nDashboard Layout\n       │\n       ├── Sidebar\n       │\n       └── Current Page\n              │\n              ├── Dashboard\n              ├── Users\n              ├── Reports\n              └── Settings\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Step 3 — Programmatic Navigation\n\n Suppose we have a button that takes a user to settings.\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function SettingsButton() {\n  const router = useRouter();\n\n  function openSettings() {\n    router.push(\"/dashboard/settings\");\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <button onClick={openSettings}>\n      Open Settings\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n This is a good example of when `useRouter()` makes sense.\n\n We aren't creating a normal text link.\n\n We're responding to an application event.\n\n---\n\n ## Step 4 — Reading Search Parameters\n\n Suppose the user visits:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n A Client Component can read:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useSearchParams } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function UserFilter() {\n  const searchParams = useSearchParams();\n\n  const role =\n    searchParams.get(\"role\") ?? \"all\";\n\n  return (\n    <p>\n      Showing users with role: {role}\n    </p>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The flow is:\n\n```\nURL\n ↓\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n ↓\nuseSearchParams()\n ↓\nrole = \"admin\"\n ↓\nUI\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Step 5 — Protecting the Dashboard with Redirect\n\n For a simplified example:\n\n```\nimport { redirect } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default async function DashboardPage() {\n  const isAuthenticated = true;\n\n  if (!isAuthenticated) {\n    redirect(\"/login\");\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <main>\n      <h1>Dashboard</h1>\n      <p>Welcome back.</p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n In a real application, `isAuthenticated` would normally come from your authentication system rather than being hard-coded.\n\n The important concept is:\n\n```\nRequest\n  ↓\nAuthentication check\n  ↓\n ┌──────────────┐\n ↓              ↓\nAuthenticated   Not authenticated\n ↓              ↓\nDashboard       redirect(\"/login\")\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Step 6 — Dynamic User Links\n\n Suppose you have:\n\n```\nconst users = [\n  { id: \"101\", name: \"Alice\" },\n  { id: \"102\", name: \"Bob\" },\n];\n```\n\n You can create links dynamically:\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nexport default function UsersPage() {\n  const users = [\n    { id: \"101\", name: \"Alice\" },\n    { id: \"102\", name: \"Bob\" },\n  ];\n\n  return (\n    <main>\n      <h1>Users</h1>\n\n      <ul>\n        {users.map((user) => (\n          <li key={user.id}>\n            <Link\n              href={`/dashboard/users/${user.id}`}\n            >\n              {user.name}\n            </Link>\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The generated destinations are:\n\n```\nAlice → /dashboard/users/101\nBob   → /dashboard/users/102\n```\n\n If you later create a dynamic route:\n\n```\napp/dashboard/users/[id]/page.tsx\n```\n\n those URLs can be handled by that dynamic route.\n\n---\n\n ## Project Data Flow\n\n By the end of this project, the navigation system looks like:\n\n```\n                    Dashboard\n                        │\n                 Dashboard Layout\n                        │\n                     Sidebar\n                        │\n        ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐\n        ↓               ↓                ↓\n    Dashboard         Users            Reports\n                        │\n                        ↓\n               Search Parameters\n                        │\n                ?role=admin\n```\n\n And for protected pages:\n\n```\nUser\n ↓\nDashboard Request\n ↓\nAuthentication Check\n ↓\n ┌──────────────┐\n ↓              ↓\nValid           Invalid\n ↓              ↓\nPage           Login\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Key Takeaways\n\n - `<Link>` is the default choice for normal navigation.\n- `useRouter()` handles navigation triggered by client-side logic.\n- `usePathname()` helps determine the current route.\n- `useSearchParams()` reads query parameters.\n- Dynamic links can be generated from application data.\n- Active navigation uses the current pathname.\n- `redirect()` can send users somewhere else based on server-side application logic.\n- Use the navigation tool that matches the situation rather than using `useRouter()` everywhere.\n\n ## Common Mistakes\n\n ### Mistake 1 — Using `useRouter()` for every link\n\n Don't turn this:\n\n```\n<Link href=\"/settings\">\n  Settings\n</Link>\n```\n\n into an unnecessary button and router call.\n\n Use `<Link>` for normal navigation.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 2 — Trying to use `useRouter()` without a Client Component\n\n If you're using the hook:\n\n```\nconst router = useRouter();\n```\n\n the component should be marked:\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 3 — Using `redirect()` inside a click handler\n\n Don't treat `redirect()` as a replacement for `router.push()` inside an interactive Client Component event handler.\n\n For a client-side event:\n\n```\nrouter.push(\"/dashboard\");\n```\n\n For appropriate server-side logic:\n\n```\nredirect(\"/dashboard\");\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 4 — Confusing route parameters and search parameters\n\n These are different:\n\n```\n/users/123\n```\n\n and:\n\n```\n/users?role=admin\n```\n\n The first uses a route segment for `123`.\n\n The second uses a search parameter for `role=admin`.\n\n---\n\n ### Mistake 5 — Forgetting that navigation state can be represented in the URL\n\n Instead of keeping every filter hidden in local state, sometimes:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n is useful because the URL can be shared, bookmarked, and interpreted by the application.  Next.js\n\n ## Mini Quiz\n\n ### Question 1\n\n Which should you normally use for a simple dashboard link?\n\n A. `redirect()`\\\n B. `useRouter()`\\\n C. `<Link>`\\\n D. `usePathname()`\n\n **Answer: C — `<Link>`**\n\n---\n\n ### Question 2\n\n Which hook tells you the current pathname?\n\n **Answer:** `usePathname()`.\n\n---\n\n ### Question 3\n\n For:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n what is the search parameter?\n\n **Answer:**\n\n```\nrole=admin\n```\n\n---\n\n ### Question 4\n\n When might `useRouter()` be appropriate?\n\n **Answer:** When client-side application logic or an event needs to trigger navigation.\n\n---\n\n ### Question 5\n\n What should happen if an authentication check fails?\n\n```\nif (!isAuthenticated) {\n  // ?\n}\n```\n\n **Answer:**\n\n```\nredirect(\"/login\");\n```\n\n when this check is being performed in an appropriate server-side context.\n\n---\n\n # Day 3 — Final Recap\n\n ## The Big Picture\n\n Today's concepts connect together like this:\n\n```\n                         Navigation\n                             │\n             ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐\n             ↓               ↓                ↓\n           <Link>         useRouter       redirect()\n             │               │                │\n       Normal links      Client logic    Server logic\n             │               │                │\n             └───────────────┼────────────────┘\n                             ↓\n                           URL\n                             │\n                  ┌──────────┴──────────┐\n                  ↓                     ↓\n             usePathname()       useSearchParams()\n                  │                     │\n                  ↓                     ↓\n            Current route          Query state\n                  │                     │\n                  └──────────┬──────────┘\n                             ↓\n                     Active / Dynamic UI\n```\n\n ## What You Should Remember\n\n 1. `<Link>` is the normal tool for internal navigation.\n2. Import `<Link>` from `next/link`.\n3. `useRouter()` is useful when code needs to trigger navigation.\n4. `useRouter()` comes from `next/navigation`.\n5. `router.push()` navigates to a route.\n6. `router.replace()` replaces the current history entry.\n7. `usePathname()` reads the current pathname.\n8. `useSearchParams()` reads query parameters.\n9. Dynamic navigation can generate links from data.\n10. Active navigation compares the current pathname with link destinations.\n11. `redirect()` is useful for server-side redirect logic in supported contexts.\n12. URLs can represent useful application state such as filters, searches, and pagination.  Next.js\n\n ## Mental Model\n\n Think of navigation as a road system.\n\n```\n                    Your Application\n                          │\n                         URLs\n                          │\n          ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐\n          ↓               ↓                ↓\n       Normal          Client           Server\n       travel          decision         decision\n          ↓               ↓                ↓\n       <Link>         useRouter()       redirect()\n```\n\n Then think of the URL as information:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n        │               │\n        │               └── Search state\n        │\n        └── Current route\n```\n\n And your application can inspect that information:\n\n```\nURL\n ↓\nusePathname()\n ↓\n\"Where am I?\"\n\nURL\n ↓\nuseSearchParams()\n ↓\n\"What filters/search values are active?\"\n```\n\n---\n\n # Final Practical Example\n\n Let's combine the day's concepts into a realistic dashboard.\n\n ## Project Structure\n\n```\napp/\n├── login/\n│   └── page.tsx\n│\n└── dashboard/\n    ├── layout.tsx\n    ├── page.tsx\n    │\n    ├── users/\n    │   ├── page.tsx\n    │   └── [id]/\n    │       └── page.tsx\n    │\n    ├── reports/\n    │   └── page.tsx\n    │\n    ├── settings/\n    │   └── page.tsx\n    │\n    └── _components/\n        ├── Sidebar.tsx\n        ├── LogoutButton.tsx\n        └── UserFilter.tsx\n```\n\n This gives us:\n\n```\n/dashboard\n/dashboard/users\n/dashboard/users/101\n/dashboard/users/102\n/dashboard/reports\n/dashboard/settings\n```\n\n And:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n can represent a filtered users page.\n\n---\n\n ## Dashboard Layout\n\n```\nimport Sidebar from \"./_components/Sidebar\";\n\nexport default function DashboardLayout({\n  children,\n}: {\n  children: React.ReactNode;\n}) {\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <Sidebar />\n\n      <main>\n        {children}\n      </main>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The layout provides the shared dashboard structure.\n\n---\n\n ## Sidebar\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\nimport { usePathname } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nconst links = [\n  {\n    name: \"Dashboard\",\n    href: \"/dashboard\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Users\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/users\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Reports\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/reports\",\n  },\n  {\n    name: \"Settings\",\n    href: \"/dashboard/settings\",\n  },\n];\n\nexport default function Sidebar() {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <aside>\n      <h2>Admin Dashboard</h2>\n\n      <nav>\n        {links.map((link) => {\n          const isActive =\n            pathname === link.href;\n\n          return (\n            <Link\n              key={link.href}\n              href={link.href}\n              aria-current={\n                isActive ? \"page\" : undefined\n              }\n            >\n              {link.name}\n            </Link>\n          );\n        })}\n      </nav>\n    </aside>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Notice how several ideas work together:\n\n```\nSidebar\n  │\n  ├── <Link>\n  │\n  ├── usePathname()\n  │\n  ├── Dynamic links\n  │\n  └── Active state\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Users Page\n\n```\nimport Link from \"next/link\";\n\nconst users = [\n  {\n    id: \"101\",\n    name: \"Alice\",\n  },\n  {\n    id: \"102\",\n    name: \"Bob\",\n  },\n];\n\nexport default function UsersPage() {\n  return (\n    <main>\n      <h1>Users</h1>\n\n      <ul>\n        {users.map((user) => (\n          <li key={user.id}>\n            <Link\n              href={`/dashboard/users/${user.id}`}\n            >\n              {user.name}\n            </Link>\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Now:\n\n```\nUsers\n │\n ├── Alice → /dashboard/users/101\n │\n └── Bob   → /dashboard/users/102\n```\n\n---\n\n ## User Filter\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useSearchParams } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function UserFilter() {\n  const searchParams = useSearchParams();\n\n  const role =\n    searchParams.get(\"role\") ?? \"all\";\n\n  return (\n    <p>\n      Current role filter: {role}\n    </p>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n For:\n\n```\n/dashboard/users?role=admin\n```\n\n the UI can display:\n\n```\nCurrent role filter: admin\n```\n\n---\n\n ## Programmatic Navigation\n\n```\n\"use client\";\n\nimport { useRouter } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default function GoToSettingsButton() {\n  const router = useRouter();\n\n  return (\n    <button\n      onClick={() =>\n        router.push(\"/dashboard/settings\")\n      }\n    >\n      Open Settings\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n Here the navigation happens because an application event triggers it.\n\n---\n\n ## Authentication Redirect\n\n At an appropriate server-side boundary:\n\n```\nimport { redirect } from \"next/navigation\";\n\nexport default async function DashboardPage() {\n  const isAuthenticated = true;\n\n  if (!isAuthenticated) {\n    redirect(\"/login\");\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <main>\n      <h1>Dashboard</h1>\n      <p>Welcome to your dashboard.</p>\n    </main>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n The complete application now has a clear navigation model:\n\n```\n                         Dashboard\n                             │\n                         Layout\n                             │\n                         Sidebar\n                             │\n          ┌──────────────────┼──────────────────┐\n          ↓                  ↓                  ↓\n      Dashboard            Users              Reports\n                              │\n                              ↓\n                         User Links\n                              │\n                  ┌───────────┴───────────┐\n                  ↓                       ↓\n              User 101                 User 102\n                  │\n                  ↓\n             Search Params\n                  │\n                  ↓\n              ?role=admin\n```\n\n And when authentication is involved:\n\n```\n                  Request\n                     ↓\n             Authentication\n                     ↓\n              ┌──────┴──────┐\n              ↓             ↓\n           Allowed        Denied\n              ↓             ↓\n          Dashboard      /login\n```\n\n That gives you the foundation for the navigation systems you'll build in larger Next.js applications: normal links, interactive navigation, active navigation, dynamic URLs, URL-based filters, and redirects.  Next.js+1";

